n simplifying the expression is to eliminate the double negation.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"Double negation law",explanation:"The double negation law says that \\\\(\\neg \\neg a\\\\) is equivalent to\\\\( a\\\\).",hint:"In this statement, \\\\(\\neg \\neg a\\\\) is rewritten as \\\\(a\\\\). What law justifies this equivalence?",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[1]},{column1:{content:"\\\\( a \\vee (b \\wedge \\neg b) \\\\)",explanation:"The expression can be simplified by pulling out the \\\\(a\\\\) from \\\\( (a \\vee b) \\\\) and \\\\( (a \\vee \\neg b) \\\\).",hint:"The expression can be simplified by pulling out the \\\\(a\\\\) from \\\\( (a \\vee b) \\\\) and \\\\( (a \\vee \\neg b) \\\\).",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"Distributive law",explanation:"The distributive law allows the \\\\(a\\\\) to be pulled out of the expression.",hint:"In this statement, \\\\(a\\\\) is pulled out of the expression \\\\( (a \\vee b) \\wedge (a \\vee \\neg b) \\\\). What law justifies this equivalence?",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[2]},{column1:{content:"\\\\( a \\vee \\text{F} \\\\)",explanation:"The expression can be simplified by replacing \\\\(b \\wedge \\neg b\\\\) which is always false.",hint:"The expression can be simplified by replacing \\\\(b \\wedge \\neg b\\\\) which is always false.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"Complement law",explanation:"The complement law says that \\\\(b \\wedge \\neg b\\\\) is equivalent to F, since \\\\(b \\wedge \\neg b\\\\) is always false.",hint:"In this statement, \\\\((b \\wedge \\neg b\\\\)) is stated as false. What law justifies this equivalence?",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[3]},{column1:{content:"\\\\( a \\\\)",explanation:"The expression can be further simplified by removing the \\\\( \\vee F \\\\) from the expression.",hint:"The expression can be further simplified by removing the \\\\( \\vee F \\\\) from the expression.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"Identity law",explanation:"The identity law says that \\\\( a \\vee F \\\\) is equivalent to \\\\( a \\\\).",hint:"In this last statement, \\\\( a \\vee \\text{F} \\\\) is rewritten as \\\\( a \\\\). What law justifies this equivalence?",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[4]}],names:{all:"proof",column1:{header:"Statements",singular:"statement"},column2:{header:"Justifications",singular:"justification"},pair:"step"},prompt:"Put the statements in the correct order to prove that \\\\( (a \\vee b) \\wedge \\neg ( \\neg a \\wedge b) \\equiv a \\\\). For each statement, also provide a justification."}},{name:"First column unmoveable: Gauss-Jordan elimination",options:{pairs:[{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{cc|c}\n-1 &amp; 2 &amp;-3 \\\\\\\\\n5 &amp; -7 &amp; 11\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:"Correct.1 The steps of the proof are in a logical order.",hint:null,moveable:!1},column2:{content:"\\\\[ 5R\\_1 + R\\_2 \\\\rightarrow R\\_2\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:"The entry below the current pivot for column \\\\(1\\\\) needs to be zero. Row \\\\(1\\\\) will be unchanged.",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[]},{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{cc|c}\n-1 &amp; 2 &amp;-3 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 3 &amp; -4\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},column2:{content:"\\\\[ \\\\begin{align}- R\\_1\\\\rightarrow R\\_1\\\\\\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} R\\_2 \\\\rightarrow R\\_2 \\\\end{align}\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:"Pivots need to be scaled to \\\\(1\\\\).",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[0]},{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{cc|c}\n1 &amp; -2 &amp; 3 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 1 &amp; -\\frac{4}{3}\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},column2:{content:"\\\\[ 2R\\_2 + R\\_1 \\\\rightarrow R\\_1\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:"The entry above the current pivot for column \\\\(2\\\\) needs to be zero. Row \\\\(2\\\\) will be unchanged.",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[1]},{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{cc|c}\n1 &amp; 0 &amp;\\frac{1}{3} \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 1 &amp; -\\frac{4}{3}\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},column2:{content:"Thus, the solution is:\n\\\\[x\\_1 = \\frac{1}{3}\\\\text{ and }x\\_2 = -\\frac{4}{3}\\\\]",explanation:"Correct. The steps are in a logical order.",hint:null,moveable:!0},dependsOn:[2]}],names:{all:"proof",column1:{header:"Augmented matrices",singular:"augmented matrix"},column2:{header:"Elementary row operations",singular:"elementary row operation"},pair:"step"},prompt:"Solve the system using Gauss-Jordan elimination.\n\\\\begin{align}\n-x\\_1 + 2x\\_2 &amp;= -3 \\\\\\\\\n 5x\\_1-7x_2 &amp;=11\n\\\\end{align}\n\n\n"}},{name:"Second column unmoveable: Gauss-Jordan elimination",options:{pairs:[{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\n1 &amp; 5 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 12 &amp; -1 &amp; -2 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; -\\frac{1}{4} &amp; \\frac{11}{2}\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:"The coefficient matrix from the system of equations is combined with the column matrix for the constants to form the augmented matrix.",hint:"The augmented matrix contains the coefficients of the system of equations and the constants in the order they appear.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"The augmented matrix needs no forward elimination steps.  Two pivots need scaling. \\\\[\\\\begin{align}\\\\frac{1}{12}  R\\\\_2 \\\\rightarrow R\\_2\\\\\\\\-4  R\\_3 \\\\rightarrow R\\_3\\\\end{align}\\\\]\n",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},dependsOn:[]},{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\n1 &amp; 5 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 1 &amp; -\\frac{1}{12} &amp; -\\frac{1}{6} \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; -22\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:"Row \\\\(2\\\\) pivot is scaled by multiplying row \\\\(2\\\\) by \\\\(\\\\frac{1}{12}\\\\), the reciprocal of the \\\\(12\\\\) pivot.\nRow \\\\(3\\\\) pivot  is scaled by multiplying row \\\\(3\\\\) by \\\\(-\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\), the reciprocal of the \\\\(-4\\\\) pivot.\n",hint:"Pivots on rows \\\\(2\\\\) and \\\\(3\\\\) should be scaled to \\\\(1\\\\). Row \\\\(1\\\\) is unchanged.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"\\\\[ \\\\dfrac{1}{12}R\\_3 + R\\_2  \\\\rightarrow R\\_2\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},dependsOn:[0]},{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\n1 &amp; 5 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; -2 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; -22\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]\n",explanation:"To get \\\\(0\\\\) in the third column of row \\\\(2\\\\), the sum of \\\\(\\\\frac{1}{12}\\\\)(row \\\\(3\\\\)) and row \\\\(2\\\\) replaces row \\\\(2\\\\).",hint:"Entries above the pivot in row \\\\(3\\\\) should be \\\\(0\\\\). Rows \\\\(1\\\\) and \\\\(3\\\\) are unchanged.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"\\\\[-5R\\_2 + R\\_1 \\\\rightarrow R\\_1\\\\]",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},dependsOn:[1]},{column1:{content:"\\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\n1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 9 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 1&amp; 0 &amp; -2 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; -22\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]\n",explanation:"To get \\\\(0\\\\) in the second column of row \\\\(1\\\\), the sum of \\\\(-5\\\\)(row \\\\(2\\\\)) and row \\\\(1\\\\) replaces row \\\\(1\\\\).",hint:"Entries above the pivot in row \\\\(2\\\\) should be \\\\(0\\\\). Rows \\\\(2\\\\) and \\\\(3\\\\) are unchanged.",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"The reduced row echelon matrix shows that\n\\\\(x\\_1 {=} 9,\\; x\\_2 {=}{-}2, \\text{ and }x\\_3 {=} {-}22\\\\)",explanation:null,hint:null,moveable:!1},dependsOn:[2]}],names:{all:"proof",column1:{header:"Augmented matrices",singular:"augmented matrix"},column2:{header:"Elementary row operations",singular:"elementary row operation"},pair:"step"},prompt:"Solve the system using Gauss-Jordan elimination.\n\\\\begin{align}\nx\\_1+5x\\_2&amp;=-1\\\\\\\\\n12x\\_2-x\\_3&amp;=-2\\\\\\\\\n-\\frac{1}{4}x_3&amp;=\\frac{11}{2}\n\\\\end{align}\n"}},{name:"Needs fullscreen",options:{pairs:Array(5).fill({column1:{content:"\\\\[ a \\\\\\\\ b \\\\\\\\ c \\\\\\\\ d \\\\]",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"short",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[]}),names:{all:"proof",column1:{header:"Statements",singular:"statement"},column2:{header:"Justifications",singular:"justification"},pair:"step"},prompt:"This needs fullscreen"}},{name:"First pair unmoveable",options:{pairs:[{column1:{content:"This is tall \\\\[\\\\left[\n\\\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\n1 &amp; 5 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; -2 \\\\\\\\\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; -22\n\\\\end{array}\\\\right]\\\\]",explanation:"explanation, pair 1, column 1",hint:"hint, pair 1, column 1",moveable:!1},column2:{content:"This is short",explanation:"explanation, pair 1, column 2",hint:"hint, pair 1, column 2",moveable:!1},dependsOn:[]},{column1:{content:"me next",explanation:"explanation, pair 2, column 1",hint:"hint, pair 2, column 1",moveable:!0},column2:{content:"then me",explanation:"explanation, pair 2, column 2",hint:"hint, pair 2, column 2",moveable:!0},dependsOn:[0]}],names:{all:"proof",column1:{header:"Statements",singular:"statement"},column2:{header:"Justifications",singular:"justification"},pair:"step"},prompt:"Meep moop"}}]})),define("content-tools/utils/options/block-ordering-pa",["exports"],(function(e){"use strict"
