e.blockOrderingPAOptions=[{name:"Direct proof (dependency bifurcation)",options:{blocks:[{content:"Let \\\\(r\\\\) be a rational number. We will show that \\\\(r^2\\\\) is also rational.",dependsOn:[],explanation:"The proof starts by stating what is assumed and what will be proven.",hint:"\\\\(r\\\\) is not defined yet."},{content:"Since \\\\(r\\\\) is rational, there exists integers \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) such that \\\\(r = \\\\frac{x}{y}\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ne 0\\\\).",dependsOn:[0],explanation:"The only available assumption is that \\\\(r\\\\) is rational, so the definition of a rational number is applied to \\\\(r\\\\) .",hint:"\\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) are not defined yet."},{content:"\\\\(r^2 = \\\\left( \\\\frac{x}{y} \\\\right)^2 = \\\\frac{x^2}{y^2}\\\\)",dependsOn:[1],explanation:"The proof must show that \\\\(r^2\\\\) is rational, so the expression \\\\(\\\\frac{x}{y}\\\\) is plugged in to \\\\(r^2\\\\). The goal of this algebra is to express \\\\(r^2\\\\) as the ratio of two integers.",hint:"Some algebra is needed for \\\\(r^2\\\\) using the expression \\\\(\\\\frac{x}{y}\\\\)."},{content:"Since \\\\(x\\\\) is an integer, \\\\(x^2\\\\) is also an integer. Since \\\\(y\\\\) is an integer, \\\\(y^2\\\\) is also an integer. Since \\\\(y \\\\ne 0\\\\), \\\\(y^2 \\\\ne 0\\\\).",dependsOn:[1],explanation:"The proof needs to argue that the derived fraction \\\\(\\\\frac{x^2}{y^2}\\\\) satisfies the requirements for a rational number: the numerator and denominator must both be integers and the denominator must be non-zero.",hint:"\\\\(x^2\\\\) and \\\\(y^2\\\\) must satisfy the requirements for rational numbers."},{content:"Since \\\\(r^2 =\\\\frac{x^2}{y^2}\\\\), \\\\(x^2\\\\) and \\\\(y^2\\\\) are both integers, and \\\\(y^2 \\\\ne 0\\\\), \\\\(r^2\\\\) is a rational number.",dependsOn:[2,3],explanation:"The proof concludes by explaining that the expression for \\\\(r^2\\\\) satisfies the requirements for a rational number, and therefore \\\\(r^2\\\\) is rational."}],orderedListName:"proof",prompt:"Theorem: The square of any rational number is also rational."}},{name:"Proof by contradition (9 steps)",options:{blocks:[{content:"Assume \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\) is rational.",dependsOn:[],explanation:"A proof by contradiction starts with the assumption that the theorem is false.",hint:"Proof by contradiction needs an assumption about \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\)."},{content:"Express \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\) as \\\\(\\\\frac{n}{d}\\\\), where \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\) are integers, \\\\(d \\\\ne 0\\\\), and there is no integer greater than \\\\(1\\\\) that divides both \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\).",dependsOn:[0],explanation:"Express the fact that \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\) is rational mathematically and reduce the resulting fraction.",hint:"The proof needs a statement defining \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\)."},{content:"\\\\(n^2 = 2d^2\\\\)",dependsOn:[1],explanation:"Square both sides of the equation \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2} = \\\\frac{n}{d}\\\\) and multiply by \\\\(d^2\\\\).",hint:"The proof needs a statement with some algebra as a consequence of \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2} = \\\\frac{n}{d}\\\\)."},{content:"\\\\(n^2\\\\) is even and therefore n is even.",dependsOn:[2],explanation:"The fact that \\\\(n^2 = 2d^2\\\\), means that \\\\(n^2\\\\) is equal to \\\\(2\\\\) times an integer and therefore \\\\(n^2\\\\) is even. Since \\\\(n^2\\\\) is even, \\\\(n\\\\) is also even.",hint:"No statement in the proof shows \\\\(n\\\\) is even."},{content:"Since \\\\(n\\\\) is even, and \\\\(n^2 = 2d^2\\\\),  then \\\\(4k^2 = 2d^2\\\\), for some integer \\\\(k\\\\).",dependsOn:[3],explanation:"If \\\\(n\\\\) is even then \\\\(n = 2k\\\\), where \\\\(k\\\\) is an integer. Plug in \\\\(2k\\\\) for \\\\(n\\\\) into \\\\(n^2 = 2d^2\\\\) to get \\\\(4k^2 = 2d^2\\\\).",hint:"No statement in the proof defines \\\\(k\\\\)."},{content:"Since \\\\(4k^2 = 2d^2\\\\) for some integer \\\\(k\\\\), \\\\(d^2\\\\) is also even.",dependsOn:[4],explanation:"Divide both sides of the equation \\\\(4k^2 = 2d^2\\\\) by \\\\(2\\\\) to conclude that \\\\(d^2\\\\) is a multiple of \\\\(2\\\\).",hint:"No statement in the proof shows \\\\(d^2\\\\) is a multiple of \\\\(2\\\\)."},{content:"Since \\\\(d^2\\\\) is even, \\\\(d\\\\) is even.",dependsOn:[5],explanation:"The previous step established that \\\\(d^2\\\\) is even. If the square of an integer is even, then the integer itself must also be even.",hint:"No statement in the proof shows \\\\(d\\\\) is even."},{content:"Since \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\) are both even, \\\\(2\\\\) divides both \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\).",dependsOn:[6],explanation:"We have now established that both \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\) are even.",hint:"No statement in the proof shows a number greater than \\\\(1\\\\) dividing both \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\)."},{content:"This contradicts the assumption that there is no integer greater than \\\\(1\\\\) that divides both \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\). Therefore \\\\(\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\) must be irrational.",dependsOn:[7],explanation:"The fact that \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\) are both divisible by \\\\(2\\\\) contradicts the assumption made at the beginning of the proof."}],orderedListName:"proof",prompt:"Theorem: \\\\(\\sqrt{2}\\\\) is an irrational number."}},{name:"Ordering the steps of a proof by cases.",options:{blocks:[{content:"Case 1: \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\)",dependsOn:[],explanation:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) are both non-negative.",hint:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) are both non-negative."},{content:"\\\\(xy \\\\ge 0\\\\) and therefore \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y| = xy\\\\). Meanwhile \\\\(|x| = x\\\\) and \\\\(|y| = y\\\\), so \\\\(|x|\\\\cdot|y| = (x)(y) = xy\\\\).",dependsOn:[0],explanation:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\).",hint:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\)."},{content:"\\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\).",dependsOn:[1],explanation:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\).",hint:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\)."},{content:"Case 2: \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\)",dependsOn:[2],explanation:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are both non-positive.",hint:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are both non-positive."},{content:"\\\\(xy \\\\ge 0\\\\) and therefore \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y| = xy\\\\). Meanwhile \\\\(|x| = -x\\\\) and \\\\(|y| = -y\\\\), so \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y| = (-x)(-y) = xy\\\\).",dependsOn:[3],explanation:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\).",hint:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\)."},{content:"\\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\).",dependsOn:[4],explanation:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\).",hint:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\)."},{content:"Case 3: \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\)",dependsOn:[5],explanation:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\(x\\) is non-negative and \\(y\\) is non-positive.",hint:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\(x\\) is non-negative and \\(y\\) is non-positive."},{content:"\\\\(xy \\\\le 0\\\\) and therefore \\\\(|x\\\\cdot y| = -xy\\\\). Meanwhile \\\\(|x| = x\\\\) and \\\\(|y| = -y\\\\), so \\\\(|x|\\\\cdot|y| = (x)(-y)= -xy\\\\).",dependsOn:[6],explanation:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\).",hint:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\)."},{content:"\\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\).",dependsOn:[7],explanation:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\).",hint:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\ge 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\le 0\\\\)."},{content:"Case 4: \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\)",dependsOn:[8],explanation:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\(x\\) is non-positive and \\(y\\) is non-positive.",hint:"Generally, the order in which cases are considered doesn't matter.\nHowever, in this proof, the first case is set to be when \\(x\\) is non-positive and \\(y\\) is non-negative."},{content:"\\\\(xy \\\\le 0\\\\) and therefore \\\\(|x\\\\cdot y| = -xy\\\\). Meanwhile \\\\(|x| = -x\\\\) and \\\\(|y| = y\\\\), so \\\\(|x|\\\\cdot|y| = (-x)(y) = -xy\\\\).",dependsOn:[9],explanation:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\).",hint:"In this step, the definition of absolute value is used to find expressions for \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y|\\\\) and \\\\(|x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) in terms of \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\)."},{content:"\\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\).",dependsOn:[10],explanation:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\).",hint:"The previous step established that \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y| = xy\\\\), so this step states concisely that the theorem is true for the case when \\\\(x \\\\le 0\\\\) and \\\\(y \\\\ge 0\\\\)."},{content:"Since the \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\) for all cases, \\\\( | x \\\\cdot y | = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\)  for all real numbers \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\).",dependsOn:[11],explanation:"The proof has shown that the theorem is true for all possible cases for \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\).",hint:"The proof has shown that the theorem is true for all possible cases for \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\)."}],orderedListName:"proof",prompt:"\n**Theorem:** For every pair of real numbers, \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\), \\\\(|x \\\\cdot y| = |x| \\\\cdot |y|\\\\).\n\nInclude the cases in the numeric order given.\n"}},{name:"Duplicate content",options:{blocks:[{content:"t",dependsOn:[],explanation:"t\\_1's explanation",hint:"t\\_1's hint"},{content:"a",dependsOn:[0],explanation:"a's explanation",hint:"a's hint"},{content:"t",dependsOn:[1],explanation:"t\\_2's explanation",hint:"t\\_2's hint"}],orderedListName:"proof",prompt:"t\\_1 -> a -> t\\_2"}}]})),define("content-tools/utils/options/char-encoding",["exports"],(function(e){"use strict"
